  "body": "[Over on Twitter](https://twitter.com/KittyGiraudel/status/1423241268646359042), Kitty Giraudel would like to select all elements that are sequentially focusable (keyboard-focusable). Using CSS selectors for this is cumbersome. It would be useful if you could just ask the browser if an element is keyboard-focusable.\r\n\r\nThere is the `tabIndex` property, but the way it is [defined](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#dom-tabindex), the value `0` does not mean that the element is keyboard-focusable, and the value `-1` does not mean that it’s not. For example, `<a>` without `href` returns the value `0`, but it’s not keyboard-focusable.\r\n\r\nWould it be possible to re-define the `tabIndex` property, so that `0` at all times matches keyboard-focusability exactly?\r\n\r\n```js\r\nif (elem.tabIndex === 0) {\r\n  // the element is currently keyboard-focusable\r\n}\r\n```",
